          %p
            This we establish in theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableHomotopyCategoryIsIndeedStabilizationOfClassicalHomotopyCategory"}
            below.
          %p
            The notation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^\infty
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\infty
            is meant to be suggestive of the intuition behind how this stabilization will work: The universal way of making a topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            become stable under suspension is to pass to its infinite suspension in a suitable sense. That suitable sense is going to be called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectrum"} suspension spectrum
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SuspensionSpectrum"}
            below). Conversely, if an object does not change up to equivalence, by forming its loop spaces, it must give an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+loop+space"} infinite loop space
          %p
            In contrast to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+category"} classical homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
            (a shadow of the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} (∞,1)-category of spectra
            is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} of chain complexes
            (of
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            gives rise to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            and every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+map"} chain map
            to a map between these spectra (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
          %p
            There is a variety of different models for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            of spectra, some of which fits into this hierarchy:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
                with their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+sequential+spectra"} model structure on sequential spectra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
                with their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+symmetric+spectra"} model structure on symmetric spectra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+spectra"} orthogonal spectra
                with their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+orthogonal+spectra"} model structure on orthogonal spectra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excisive+functors"} excisive functors
                with their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+excisive+functors"} model structure for excisive functors
          %p As one moves down this list, the objects modelling the spectra become richer. This means on the one hand that their abstract properties become better as one moves down the list, on the other hand it means that it is more immediate to construct and manipulate examples as one stays further up in the list.
          %p
            We start with plain sequential spectra as a transparent means to construct the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            it is convenient to first pass to the richer model of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+spectra"} highly structured spectra
            %em
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1-2"} Part II
          %p
            The most lighweight model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            in a straightforward way, and have the advantage that examples tend to be immediate (for instance the proof of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            spits out sequential spectra).
          %p
            The key disadvantage of sequential spectra is that they do not support a functorial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product+of+spectra"} smash product of spectra
            before passing to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric smash product of spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            %a{:href => "#SymmetricSpectra"} below
            we enhance sequential spectra to the more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+spectrum"} highly structured
            models given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+spectra"} orthogonal spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free-forgetful+adjunction"} free-forgetful adjunction
            and for working with either it is useful to have the means to pass back and forth between them.
          %h2#sequential_prespectra Sequential pre-spectra
          %p
            The following def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SequentialSpectra"}
            is the traditional component-wise definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "spectrum#Lima58"} Lima 58
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "spectrum#Boardman65"} Boardman 65
          %blockquote
            %p
              It is generally supposed that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Whitehead"} G. W. Whitehead
              also had something to do with it, but the latter takes a modest attitude about that. (
              %a{:href => "#Adams74"}> Adams 74, p. 131
              )
          %p
            Below in prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SequentialSpectraAsDiagramSpectra"}
            we discuss an equivalent definition of sequential spectra as “topological diagram spectra” (
            %a{:href => "#MMSS00"}> Mandell-May-Schwede-Shipley 00
            ), namely as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+functors"} topologically enriched functors
            (
            %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#TopologicallyEnrichedFunctor"}> defn.
            ) on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topologically+enriched+category"} topologically enriched category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+model+category"} stable model category
            structure (
            %a{:href => "#StableModelStructureOnSequentialSpectra"}> below
            ) and for establishing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric monoidal smash product of spectra
            (in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DiagramSpectra"} 1.2
          %p
            Throughout, our ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Top
                      %mi cg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top_{cg}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
            (
            %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#kTop"}> defn.
            ).
          #SequentialSpectra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectrum"} sequential
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prespectrum"} prespectrum
                  in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectrum"} sequential spectrum
              for short (or even just
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"}> spectrum
                ), is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  an
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+space"} pointed
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo •
                        %mo =
