            %p
              The fact that the second horizontal morphism from below is indeed an isomorphism follows by applying the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
              for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushouts"} homotopy pushouts
              twice (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#ClosurePropertiesOfHomotopyPullbacks"}> prop.
              ):
            %div{:style => "float:left;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
              %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/OctahedralProofFromPastingPushouts.jpg", :width => "400"}/
            %p
              Draw all homotopy cofibers as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
              squares (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#HomotopyPullback"}> def.
              ) with one edge going to the point. Then assemble the squares (1)-(3) in the pasting composite of two cubes on top of each other: (1) as the left face of the top cube, (2) as the middle face where the two cubes touch, and (3) as the front face of the bottom cube. All remaining edges are points. This way the rear and front face of the top cube and the left and right face of the bottom cube are homotopy pushouts by construction. Also the top face
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mo ≃
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp;\longrightarrow &amp; \ast
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{\simeq}}\downarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\downarrow
                    \\\
                    A &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \ast
                    }
            %p
              is a homotopy pushout, since two opposite edges of it are weak equivalences (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#HomotopyPullbackOfWeakEquivalences"}> prop.
              ). From this the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
              for homotopy pushouts (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#ClosurePropertiesOfHomotopyPullbacks"}> prop.
              ) gives that also the middle square (2) is a homotopy pushout. Applying the pasting law once more this way, now for the bottom cube, gives that the bottom square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi D
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi D
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\ast &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \ast
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    D/B &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; (D/A)/(B/A)
                    }
            %p
              is a homotopy pushout. Since here the left edge is a weak equivalence, necessarily, so is the right edge (
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#HomotopyPullbackOfWeakEquivalences"}> prop.
              ), which hence exhibits the claimed identification
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D/B \simeq (D/A)/(B/A)
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_11 Remark
            %p
              All we used in the proof (of prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableHomotopyCategoryIsTriangulated"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octahedral+axiom"} octahedral axiom
              (T4) is the existence and nature of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushouts"} homotopy pushouts
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Hubery"} Hubery
          %h3#long_fibercofiber_sequences Long fiber-cofiber sequences
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            there are generally long
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequences"} homotopy fiber sequences
            to the left and long
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+cofiber+sequences"} homotopy cofiber sequences
            to the right, as discussed in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#LongSequences"} Homotopy theory – Long sequences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            (since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            is triangulated by prop.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StableHomotopyCategoryIsTriangulated"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            both these sequences are long in both directions, and in fact coincide.
          %p
            %strong Literature
            (
            %a{:href => "#Schwede12"}> Schwede 12, II.2
            )
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          #CompositesInADistinguishedTriangleAreZero.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_20 Lemma
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi CofSeq
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ho,\Sigma, CofSeq)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+category"} triangulated category
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithCofiberSequences"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi f
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi g
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi h
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    A \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} B \overset{g}{\longrightarrow} B/A \overset{h}{\longrightarrow} \Sigma A
            %p a distinguished triangle, then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g\circ f = 0
            %p
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphism"} zero morphism
          .proof
            %h6#proof_53 Proof
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi id
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi g
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi h
                          %mtd
                            %mi Σ
                            %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A
                    \&amp;\overset{id}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    A
                    \&amp;\overset{}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    0
                    \&amp;\overset{}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    \\Sigma A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{id}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    A
                    \&amp;\overset{f}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    B
                    \&amp;\overset{g}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    B/A
                    \&amp;\overset{h}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    \\Sigma A
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Observe that the top part is a distinguished triangle by axioms T1 and T2 in def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CategoryWithCofiberSequences"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
